window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-37"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "The framing has a constraint in it that is doing real work, so I want to check I have read it right before I write anything: no index over the file. That is what makes this a sorted-array sweep rather than a hash map, and if I have that wrong I am about to solve a different problem.", "questions": ["The file is memory-mapped and I cannot build an index over it. Does that budget cover a hash map keyed on amount, which is what I would reach for otherwise?", "Two entries carrying the same amount: is that one explanation or two? It changes what the answer set even is.", "Is the file guaranteed sorted by amount when I get it, and are ties broken by anything?", "What is the largest group anyone actually asks for? That is a correctness question here, not a performance one."], "assumptions": ["The file arrives sorted ascending by amount with ties broken by id, and a file that is not is a fault rather than something to sort in memory.", "The memory budget forbids an index over the file: cursors and the answer being built, and nothing proportional to the file.", "Sets naming the same amounts are one explanation, reported once and named by the earliest entries carrying those amounts.", "Amounts are signed minor units, and a magnitude ceiling is what keeps sums inside an Int."], "edgeCases": ["a file too short to hold the requested group, where there is no answer rather than an empty one", "a file of all-identical amounts, where every group is the same explanation", "a target far outside anything the file can reach, where the nearest question still has an answer", "a group size of zero or larger than the file, both of which are caller bugs"], "restate": "Over a sorted, memory-mapped day-file that may not be indexed, find the sets of entries that net to a target discrepancy: pairs within a range and the nearest miss, triples, the nearest triple, and the general k-entry case."}, "solution": {"pitch": "The sweep itself is the easy half. What makes this problem is that the sweep is a subroutine rather than the whole algorithm, so the decision that matters happens at the signature and not in the loop. I write one converging sweep that takes a range and returns both the exact hits and the closest miss, and then every other part is an outer enumeration that calls it: triples fix one entry and read the hits, the nearest triple fixes one entry and reads the miss, and the general case is a recursion that bottoms out in exactly the same call. Sortedness pays twice here, once for the sweep and once for making duplicate suppression a comparison against the neighbour rather than a set of tuples.", "dataStructures": ["Two integer cursors and the results being accumulated. No index over the file, which is the constraint the whole design is built around.", "A range argument rather than a sliced array, so handing a suffix down costs nothing and copies nothing.", "The recursion's own stack, which is bounded by the group size rather than by the file.", "An immutable magnitude ceiling as a static constant, which is what lets the sweep do its arithmetic without a guard."], "types": ["The pair scan is its own type with both the exact matches and the nearest miss, because that decision is the whole composition story.", "A set of entries carries its ids and its total rather than indices, since the caller's next step is to go and look at the entries.", "Failures are a typed enum: an out-of-order file names the index, an out-of-range amount names the ceiling, and an impossible group size says which way it was impossible.", "The group size ceiling and the amount ceiling are separate constants, because they exist for different reasons and are read by different people."], "composition": "Every part calls the first one. Triples fix an entry and read the exact matches over the suffix; the nearest triple fixes an entry and reads the closest miss over the same suffix; the general reduction recurses until two entries are left and then makes exactly that call. Because the calls are real rather than decorative, the suite can assert them: groups of three must equal the triples, and groups of two must equal a scan over the whole file. Those two equalities can be red, which is what makes them worth asserting rather than describing.", "tradeoff": "Returning the nearest miss alongside the hits makes the first part's return type larger than the first question needs. That is the trade, and it is the right way round: a method returning only hits cannot answer how close the best pair came, so the nearest question would have to re-run the entire sweep with a different accumulator, which is the same algorithm written twice with two places for a bug to live. Carrying both costs one comparison per step while I am already there.", "trap": "Duplicate suppression in three places that are each easy to get right alone: the outer enumeration skips a repeated amount before starting a sweep, and the sweep skips repeated amounts on both sides after recording a hit. Getting the outer one right and the inner ones wrong reports the same amounts twice; the reverse misses valid sets. The second trap is Swift-specific and fatal rather than wrong: Int addition traps in release as well as debug, so an unguarded sum over large minor units ends the process instead of returning a bad number, and it takes every other suite in the package down with it."}, "coding": {"order": ["Write the file's preconditions first: sorted by amount with ties by id, and every amount inside the magnitude ceiling.", "Write the sweep next, with the range argument and both halves of the return in place from the start rather than retrofitted.", "Test it on a file with a repeated amount before going any further, because every later part inherits whatever the skips do here.", "Write the triples as an outer loop calling the sweep, and add the outer duplicate skip deliberately rather than after seeing a duplicate.", "Write the nearest triple next; if it is more than a short loop, go back and look at the return type instead of pushing on.", "Write the general reduction last, stopping at two rather than three, and check it against the two parts it generalises."], "commentary": ["I cannot index the file, so the sweep is what sortedness buys me: under the target I give up the smallest amount in play, over it I give up the largest.", "I am returning the closest miss as well as the hits, and that is deliberate. The nearest-triple question needs it, and a hits-only answer would make that part re-run this whole sweep.", "The range argument is the other half of that decision: it is what lets the later parts fix an entry and hand down the suffix without copying anything.", "After a hit I step both cursors past every entry carrying the amounts I just used. Two entries of the same amount are one explanation for a reconciler.", "A hit can only land on a block boundary, because moving inside a run of equal amounts leaves the sum unchanged. So I name the first entry of each amount rather than wherever the cursor stopped.", "The triple case fixes an entry and asks the pair question of the suffix. The outer skip is the same argument as the inner ones, one level up.", "The nearest triple is the same enumeration reading the other field. Nothing about the sweep is rewritten, which is exactly what the return type bought.", "The general case bottoms out at two entries left, not three, or I would have a second copy of the triple logic inside the recursion.", "The magnitude ceiling is checked when the file is opened, so the sweep needs no overflow guard. Swift traps on Int overflow in release too, and a trap ends the process rather than failing a test."], "checkIn": "Before writing the sweep I would say: I am going to return both the exact matches and the closest miss from this, even though you have only asked for the matches. The reason is that the nearest-set question is coming and a matches-only answer would make me write this loop twice. If you would rather see the smaller signature first, say so now, because retrofitting it later is the expensive direction."}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Walk a six-entry file of minus four, minus one, minus one, nothing, one and two against a target of one: the sweep finds minus one with two, and nothing with one.", "Walk the same file against a target of nothing: minus one with one is the only pair, once rather than twice, because the second entry carrying minus one is the same explanation.", "Walk the triples of that file against a target of nothing: minus one with minus one and two, and minus one with nothing and one.", "Walk the nearest triple against a target of a hundred: nothing, one and two total three, and nothing in the file gets closer.", "Walk a file of minus five followed by three zeros and confirm the pair netting to nothing names the first two zeros, not the last two."], "tests": "Run the finder's suite and read the four parts in order. The repeated-amount fixtures are what catch the skips, and the file of minus five followed by three zeros is what catches naming the wrong witness. The fourth suite asserts the composition directly: groups of three equal the triples and groups of two equal a whole-file scan, on every fixture and every target. Every part is also checked against an independently written enumeration of all combinations, which shares none of the recurrence's machinery.", "complexity": "The sweep is linear in the range it is given after the file is already sorted, and the reduction is one order of magnitude worse per extra entry: triples are quadratic, the general case is the file's length to the power of the group size minus one. Space is the recursion depth and the answer, and nothing else, which is the constraint the framing set. The alternative worth naming out loud is the hash index: it makes the pair question linear, and it is exactly what the memory budget forbids.", "moreTime": "Netting with a tolerance rather than an exact target, which turns each hit into a band and changes what the cursors may discard. Weighted or signed constraints, such as requiring at least one credit and one debit in every set, which prunes the enumeration rather than the sweep. And streaming the file in pages, where the sweep survives but the recursion's suffix handling does not, which is the honest limit of this design."}, "code": "public struct LedgerEntry: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let id: String\n    public let amountMinor: Int\n\n    public init(id: String, amountMinor: Int) {\n        self.id = id\n        self.amountMinor = amountMinor\n    }\n}\n\npublic struct EntrySet: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let entryIDs: [String]\n    public let total: Int\n\n    public init(entryIDs: [String], total: Int) {\n        self.entryIDs = entryIDs\n        self.total = total\n    }\n}\n\npublic struct PairScan: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let exactMatches: [EntrySet]\n    public let nearest: EntrySet?\n\n    public init(exactMatches: [EntrySet], nearest: EntrySet?) {\n        self.exactMatches = exactMatches\n        self.nearest = nearest\n    }\n}\n\npublic enum NettingError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case unsortedLedger(index: Int)\n    case nonPositiveGroupSize(Int)\n    case groupSizeExceedsLedger(Int)\n    case groupSizeExceedsSupportedMaximum(k: Int, maximum: Int)\n    case amountRangeUnsupported(maxAbsoluteAmount: Int)\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\npublic struct NettingFinder: Sendable {\n    /// The largest group this finder will assemble. Sums of up to this many\n    /// amounts are what the range guard below is sized against.\n    public static let maxGroupSize = 8\n\n    /// The ceiling on a single entry's magnitude. It is a machine limit rather\n    /// than an accounting one: Swift traps on `Int` overflow in debug and in\n    /// release alike, so a sum that does not fit ends the process instead of\n    /// returning a wrong number. Bounding a single amount here is what makes\n    /// every sum of up to `maxGroupSize` amounts safe without a check inside\n    /// the sweep.\n    public static let maxAbsoluteAmount = Int.max / (maxGroupSize + 1)\n\n    private let ledger: [LedgerEntry]\n\n    public init(ledger: [LedgerEntry]) throws(NettingError) {\n        for (index, entry) in ledger.enumerated() {\n            guard entry.amountMinor.magnitude <= UInt(Self.maxAbsoluteAmount) else {\n                throw .amountRangeUnsupported(maxAbsoluteAmount: Self.maxAbsoluteAmount)\n            }\n            guard index > 0 else { continue }\n            let previous = ledger[index - 1]\n            let ordered = previous.amountMinor < entry.amountMinor\n                || (previous.amountMinor == entry.amountMinor && previous.id <= entry.id)\n            guard ordered else { throw .unsortedLedger(index: index) }\n        }\n        self.ledger = ledger\n    }\n\n    /// The day-file as read, for a caller that wants to check what it handed in.\n    public var entries: [LedgerEntry] { ledger }\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - Scan one range for pairs\n\n    /// Every pair inside `range` whose amounts net to `target`, plus the pair\n    /// that came closest whether or not any of them landed.\n    ///\n    /// Two cursors converge from the ends of the range. Sortedness is what\n    /// makes that legal: when the running sum is under the target the only way\n    /// to raise it is to give up the smallest amount still in play, and when it\n    /// is over, the only way to lower it is to give up the largest.\n    ///\n    /// The nearest pair rides along rather than being computed by a second\n    /// sweep, because the callers in later parts need it. A method that\n    /// returned only the exact matches would force each of them to re-run this\n    /// whole sweep with a different accumulator, which is the same algorithm\n    /// written twice.\n    ///\n    /// Two sets naming the same amounts are one explanation for a reconciler,\n    /// so a repeated amount is consumed once: after a hit, both cursors step\n    /// past every entry carrying the amount they just used, and every reported\n    /// set names the earliest entries carrying its amounts.\n    public func scanPairs(summingTo target: Int, in range: Range<Int>) -> PairScan {\n        var lo = max(range.lowerBound, 0)\n        var hi = min(range.upperBound, ledger.count) - 1\n\n        var exactMatches: [EntrySet] = []\n        var nearest: EntrySet?\n\n        while lo < hi {\n            let sum = ledger[lo].amountMinor + ledger[hi].amountMinor\n\n            if isCloser(sum, than: nearest, to: target) {\n                nearest = set(at: [lo, earliestWitness(of: hi, after: lo)], total: sum)\n            }\n\n            if sum == target {\n                exactMatches.append(set(at: [lo, earliestWitness(of: hi, after: lo)], total: sum))\n\n                let lowAmount = ledger[lo].amountMinor\n                let highAmount = ledger[hi].amountMinor\n                while lo < hi, ledger[lo].amountMinor == lowAmount { lo += 1 }\n                while lo < hi, ledger[hi].amountMinor == highAmount { hi -= 1 }\n            } else if sum < target {\n                lo += 1\n            } else {\n                hi -= 1\n            }\n        }\n\n        return PairScan(exactMatches: exactMatches, nearest: nearest)\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - Triples\n\n    /// Every distinct set of three entries netting to `target`.\n    ///\n    /// The sweep from Part 1 becomes the inner loop. Fixing the first entry\n    /// turns the question into a pair question over the suffix after it, which\n    /// is the whole reduction, and the range argument is what lets that suffix\n    /// be handed down without copying the file.\n    ///\n    /// Duplicate suppression happens in two places and both are needed. The\n    /// outer loop skips an amount equal to the one it just used, because that\n    /// entry would ask the same question of a shorter suffix and get back a\n    /// subset of the same answers. The inner skips live in Part 1.\n    public func triples(summingTo target: Int) -> [EntrySet] {\n        var results: [EntrySet] = []\n        guard ledger.count >= 3 else { return results }\n\n        for index in 0...(ledger.count - 3) {\n            if index > 0, ledger[index].amountMinor == ledger[index - 1].amountMinor { continue }\n\n            let scan = scanPairs(\n                summingTo: residual(of: target, minus: ledger[index].amountMinor),\n                in: (index + 1)..<ledger.count\n            )\n            for pair in scan.exactMatches {\n                results.append(prepending(index, to: pair))\n            }\n        }\n\n        return results\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - Nearest triple when nothing nets exactly\n\n    /// The set of three entries whose total comes closest to `target`.\n    ///\n    /// This is the same outer enumeration as Part 2 reading the other half of\n    /// what Part 1 already returns. Nothing about the sweep is rewritten, which\n    /// is exactly what the richer return type bought.\n    ///\n    /// Ties resolve to the lower total, and then to the entries that appear\n    /// earlier in the file, so two runs over one day-file cannot disagree.\n    public func closestTriple(to target: Int) -> EntrySet? {\n        guard ledger.count >= 3 else { return nil }\n\n        var best: EntrySet?\n        for index in 0...(ledger.count - 3) {\n            if index > 0, ledger[index].amountMinor == ledger[index - 1].amountMinor { continue }\n\n            let scan = scanPairs(\n                summingTo: residual(of: target, minus: ledger[index].amountMinor),\n                in: (index + 1)..<ledger.count\n            )\n            guard let pair = scan.nearest else { continue }\n\n            let candidate = prepending(index, to: pair)\n            if isCloser(candidate.total, than: best, to: target) {\n                best = candidate\n            }\n        }\n\n        return best\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 4 - The general k-entry reduction\n\n    /// Every distinct set of exactly `k` entries netting to `target`.\n    ///\n    /// The reduction is the point: a k-sum is a (k - 1)-sum over each suffix,\n    /// and the recursion bottoms out in exactly one call to the Part 1 sweep\n    /// when two entries are left to place. There is one sweep in this file and\n    /// four questions asked of it.\n    public func groups(ofSize k: Int, summingTo target: Int) throws(NettingError) -> [EntrySet] {\n        guard k > 0 else { throw .nonPositiveGroupSize(k) }\n        guard k <= Self.maxGroupSize else {\n            throw .groupSizeExceedsSupportedMaximum(k: k, maximum: Self.maxGroupSize)\n        }\n        guard k <= ledger.count else { throw .groupSizeExceedsLedger(k) }\n\n        return reduce(k: k, summingTo: target, from: 0)\n    }\n\n    // MARK: The reduction, and the pieces every part shares\n\n    private func reduce(k: Int, summingTo target: Int, from start: Int) -> [EntrySet] {\n        if k == 2 {\n            return scanPairs(summingTo: target, in: start..<ledger.count).exactMatches\n        }\n        if k == 1 {\n            guard let index = (start..<ledger.count).first(where: { ledger[$0].amountMinor == target })\n            else { return [] }\n            return [set(at: [index], total: target)]\n        }\n\n        var results: [EntrySet] = []\n        var index = start\n        while index <= ledger.count - k {\n            defer { index += 1 }\n            if index > start, ledger[index].amountMinor == ledger[index - 1].amountMinor { continue }\n\n            let deeper = reduce(\n                k: k - 1,\n                summingTo: residual(of: target, minus: ledger[index].amountMinor),\n                from: index + 1\n            )\n            for group in deeper {\n                results.append(prepending(index, to: group))\n            }\n        }\n\n        return results\n    }\n\n    private func set(at indices: [Int], total: Int) -> EntrySet {\n        EntrySet(entryIDs: indices.map { ledger[$0].id }, total: total)\n    }\n\n    private func prepending(_ index: Int, to group: EntrySet) -> EntrySet {\n        EntrySet(\n            entryIDs: [ledger[index].id] + group.entryIDs,\n            total: ledger[index].amountMinor + group.total\n        )\n    }\n\n    /// The target the rest of a group has to reach once one entry is fixed.\n    ///\n    /// A caller may pass any `Int` as a target, and the entry amounts are\n    /// bounded but the target is not, so this subtraction is the one place in\n    /// the file where overflow is reachable. Saturating is the right answer\n    /// rather than trapping or wrapping: a residual that does not fit in an\n    /// `Int` is one no bounded group of amounts can ever reach, and the\n    /// saturated value behaves exactly like the unreachable target it stands\n    /// for, including for the nearest-miss question.\n    private func residual(of target: Int, minus amount: Int) -> Int {\n        let (value, overflowed) = target.subtractingReportingOverflow(amount)\n        guard overflowed else { return value }\n        return amount < 0 ? Int.max : Int.min\n    }\n\n    /// How far a total sits from the target, saturating rather than trapping\n    /// for the same reason the residual does.\n    private func distance(_ total: Int, to target: Int) -> Int {\n        let (value, overflowed) = total.subtractingReportingOverflow(target)\n        guard !overflowed, value != Int.min else { return Int.max }\n        return value < 0 ? -value : value\n    }\n\n    /// The earliest entry after `lo` carrying the same amount as `index`.\n    ///\n    /// A hit can only land on a block boundary, because advancing a cursor\n    /// inside a run of equal amounts leaves the sum unchanged. So the low\n    /// cursor is already on the first entry carrying its amount while the high\n    /// cursor is on the last entry carrying its own, and naming the first of\n    /// both is what makes a reported set the earliest witness rather than\n    /// whichever entry the sweep happened to stop on.\n    private func earliestWitness(of index: Int, after lo: Int) -> Int {\n        let amount = ledger[index].amountMinor\n        var witness = index\n        while witness - 1 > lo, ledger[witness - 1].amountMinor == amount {\n            witness -= 1\n        }\n        return witness\n    }\n\n    /// The nearest-miss ordering, stated once so Parts 1 and 3 cannot drift:\n    /// closer wins, then the lower total, then the entries that appear earlier\n    /// in the file, which the sweep and the outer enumeration both reach first.\n    private func isCloser(_ total: Int, than current: EntrySet?, to target: Int) -> Bool {\n        guard let current else { return true }\n\n        let candidate = distance(total, to: target)\n        let incumbent = distance(current.total, to: target)\n        if candidate != incumbent { return candidate < incumbent }\n        return total < current.total\n    }\n}\n"};
